  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nCanadian high pressure over the northern and central Great Lakes\nthis afternoon through early tonight will briefly keep the\nactive frontal boundary to our south near the Ohio River.\nHowever, the current showers and thunderstorms over Illinois and\nWisconsin are a sign of what's to come for the weekend. This\nactivity is tied to the first in a series of mid/upper shortwave\ntroughs that will progress across the southern Great Lakes \ntonight through Sunday. Increasing isentropic ascent and right \nentrance forcing beneath a subtle upper jet streak will generate\nshowers near Lake Erie and NW Ohio by Saturday morning, but \nabundant low-level dry air will likely cause much of this \ninitial activity to fall apart. Kept chance to slight chance \nPOPS in the morning and afternoon for low impact, scattered \nshowers.\n\nBetter potential for more of a widespread, heavier rain will\nstart to arrive Saturday evening and especially Saturday night\nthrough Sunday morning as the next and much stronger mid/upper\nshortwave crosses the southern Great Lakes region. An 80+ knot\nupper jet streak rounding the base of the shortwave trough and \nupper diffluence will aid in a slightly deepening surface low\ntracking from NE Iowa Saturday afternoon through southern\nOntario Saturday night/early Sunday. The frontal boundary will\nlift back north into the region ahead of this low Saturday\nafternoon and Saturday night, likely becoming quasi-stationary\nnear the south shore of Lake Erie. This will bring the rich\nlow-level moisture characterized by 70+ F dew points back into\nthe area. The combination of strong warm/moist advection and\nisentropic ascent north of the lifting frontal boundary, upper\ndiffluence and right entrance forcing south of the\naforementioned jet streak tracking over Lower Michigan, and the\nmoisture rich airmass will lead to a band of steady rain. There\nwill be a \"sweet spot\" where the strongest jet dynamics align to\ntighten mid-level frontogenesis, and that is where an embedded\nnarrower strip of heavier convection is expected to line up.\nMost of the region will see 0.50 to 1.00 inch of rain late\nSaturday through midday Sunday, but where the embedded band of\nheavier convection sets up could see 1-2 inches (locally\nhigher). PWATs of 1.75 to 2 inches will be in the 90th to 99.5th\npercentile of daily climatology, and this combined with deep \nwarm cloud layers supports efficient rainfall rates. The frontal\nboundary will be aligned fairly parallel to the mid/upper flow,\nso will need to watch for training convection where this narrow\nband sets up. This could lead to rises on area rivers, creeks, \nand streams as well as flooding of typical flood prone area. The\nWPC Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall still looks good for \nboth Saturday and Sunday (Level 2 of 4) in NW Ohio since \nconsensus among RRFS and HREF CAMS are showing greatest \nconsistency over that area, but this heavy rain swath could also\nextend across the lakeshore counties of NE Ohio and Erie \nCounty, PA. Held off on a Flood Watch because there is still \nplenty of time to see future CAMS pinpoint the exact location of\nthe convective band, but a Watch could be considered in the \nnext couple of forecast cycles.\n\nThe area will dry out from north to south Sunday afternoon and\nSunday night as a strong cold front pushes south of the region\nbehind the exiting surface low and Canadian high pressure tries\nto build in from the northern Great Lakes.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe first true stretch of Fall weather will arrive Monday and\npersist through next week as a broad mid/upper trough across\nthe Great Lakes becomes blocked to the south of an anomalous\nmid/upper ridge building over most of Canada. This will result\nin a closed (cut off) low eventually developing and pinwheeling\nbetween the Ohio Valley and Mid Atlantic regions mid to late \nweek to the south of a strong Canadian high over eastern Canada.\nThis pattern will result in cool NE low-level flow and breezy \nconditions, along with periods of rain showers. Details for each\nday are very uncertain at this time, but the main message is \ncool with periods of showers all week. Highs will generally be \nin the low/upper 60s with lows in the 50s.",
